╭─ STRIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  A white-box security assessment of the `profile-wired` codebase was         │
│  completed. The scope is a minimal local Git repository whose entire         │
│  content is a two-line Python function (`add(a,b)` returning `a+b`) and a    │
│  one-line README.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overall risk posture:** No risk identified. No vulnerabilities were       │
│  found.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Key findings**                                                            │
│  - No security vulnerabilities exist in this codebase. No reports were       │
│  filed.                                                                      │
│  - The repository has no application runtime surface: no web server,         │
│  routes, request handlers, persistence, outbound requests, or CLI            │
│  entrypoint.                                                                 │
│  - It declares no dependencies, so there is no supply-chain exposure to      │
│  assess.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **Business impact**                                                         │
│  - None. There is no code path in this repository through which an attacker  │
│  could access, modify, or disrupt anything. The absence of findings          │
│  reflects an absence of attack surface, not a failure of testing coverage:   │
│  the entire contents, including full Git history and object database, were   │
│  enumerated and read.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Assessment type: **white-box static source review** (no dynamic testing     │
│  applicable — the codebase defines no runnable service).                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **Scope:** the complete contents of the `profile-wired` Git repository —    │
│  tracked files, all Git refs, the full object database, working-tree state   │
│  including untracked and ignored files.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Activities performed**                                                    │
│  - Full source read and structural (AST-level) analysis of every committed   │
│  file.                                                                       │
│  - Complete history and inventory enumeration: all refs, every object in     │
│  the object database, unreachable/dangling object recovery, stash, tags,     │
│  submodules, reflog, and byte-level comparison of working-tree files         │
│  against committed blobs to detect uncommitted or masking content.           │
│  - Repository-wide pattern sweep for injection, execution, deserialization,  │
│  cryptographic, credential, and I/O constructs.                              │
│  - Supply-chain review (manifests, lockfiles, vendored code, imports).       │
│  - Independent re-verification by a second reviewer applying the same        │
│  methodology, explicitly attempting to falsify the initial inventory and     │
│  conclusion.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Frameworks referenced:** OWASP Top 10 and OWASP WSTG categories were      │
│  used to structure coverage (injection, broken access control,               │
│  authentication, cryptographic failures, SSRF, deserialization, and          │
│  dependency risk).                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Constraints:** the repository's configured remote origin is a local path  │
│  that no longer exists, so no upstream comparison was possible. This does    │
│  not affect the conclusion, since the local object database was fully and    │
│  self-consistently enumerated.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Severity model:** exploitability combined with demonstrated impact. No    │
│  candidate reached the reporting bar because no attacker-reachable code      │
│  path exists.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Contents assessed**                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  The repository holds exactly two files across all refs:                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=1 endLineNumber=2                    │
│  def add(a,b):                                                               │
│      return a+b                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```text title=README.md startLineNumber=1 endLineNumber=1                   │
│  # tiny                                                                      │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Verification detail**                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  - The object database contains exactly four objects (two blobs, one tree,   │
│  one commit), all reachable; no dangling or unreachable objects, no stash    │
│  entries, no tags, no submodules, and no untracked or ignored files.         │
│  - Working-tree bytes are identical to the committed blobs, confirming no    │
│  uncommitted or masking content.                                             │
│  - History is a single commit; reflog shows only the initial clone.          │
│  - No imports, no evaluation or execution calls, no network, file, or        │
│  database access, and no credential material appear anywhere in the          │
│  repository.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Why vulnerability classes do not apply**                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Every applicable class — SQL injection, XSS, SSRF, object-level             │
│  authorization, authentication and token weaknesses, deserialization, path   │
│  traversal, file upload, command injection, template injection, XXE, CSRF,   │
│  request smuggling, race conditions, business logic, and dependency CVEs —   │
│  depends on a runtime surface, data store, trust boundary, or third-party    │
│  component. None of these are present. `add()` has no caller within the      │
│  repository and performs only Python's built-in `+` operator; with no entry  │
│  point supplying operands, there is no attacker-reachable input and thus no  │
│  vulnerability. This is a property of the snippet's design, not a weakness,  │
│  and it was deliberately not reported.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Attack chaining:** not applicable. With zero confirmed findings and no    │
│  reachable primitive, there are no combinations to chain. All candidate      │
│  combinations were ruled out as unrelated by construction rather than by     │
│  omission.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  **Systemic themes:** none. There is no application architecture in scope.   │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Immediate**                                                               │
│  1. No remediation is required for this codebase; there are no findings to   │
│  address.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  **Short-term**                                                              │
│  1. If this snippet is intended to become part of a real service, re-assess  │
│  the actual application once request-handling code exists. Any security      │
│  review of `add()` is only meaningful in the context of a caller that        │
│  supplies its arguments, so the trust boundary should be re-derived from     │
│  that code rather than from this repository.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Medium-term**                                                             │
│  1. Adopt standard engineering hygiene for any repository that grows beyond  │
│  a snippet: maintain declared dependency manifests so supply-chain scanning  │
│  is possible, and keep a `SECURITY.md` documenting the intended deployment   │
│  context and contact path for vulnerability reports.                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Retest and validation**                                                   │
│  No retest is needed; the assessment covered the entire repository,          │
│  including its complete history and object database, and was independently   │
│  corroborated.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
